    "title": "What is a red flag warning? Ohio counties at 'critical' risk for wildfires. Here's where",
    "text": "March 19, 2025, 9:21 a.m. ETSmokers, don't toss that lit cigarette from the car window. The National Weather Service warns that it could spark a wildfire, especially today.The NWS has issued a red flag warning from 11 a.m. to 10 p.m. today for several counties from Central to Southeast Ohio. Here's what that means.What is a red flag warning? National Weather Service definitionA red flag warning means that warm temperatures, very low humidity and strong winds are expected to combine to create an increased risk of fire danger, according to the NWS.Per the NWS warning issued today, south winds of 15 to 20 mph with gusts up to 35, humidity as low as 14%, temperatures of up to 79 degrees and moisture in wood of 5-8% will create \"critical fire weather conditions.\"On federal land, red flag warnings alert fire managers that conditions are bad for prescribed burns and may lead to \"dangerous wildfire growth.\"What Ohio counties are under a red flag warning?The following Ohio counties are under a red flag warning today:AdamsBelmontCarrollColumbianaCoshoctonGuernseyFairfieldHarrisonHockingJeffersonLewisLickingMonroeMuskingumNoblePikeRossSciotoTuscarawasWhat to do if you are under a red flag warning?To fight that risk, the NWS advises:If you are allowed to burn in your area, all burn barrels must use a weighted metal cover, with holes no larger than three-quarters of an inch.Do not throw cigarettes or matches out of a moving vehicle. They may ignite dry grass on the side of the road that can become a wildfire.Extinguish all outdoor fires properly. Drown fires with plenty of water and stir the ashes to make sure everything is cold to the touch. Dunk charcoal in water until cold. Do not throw live charcoal on the ground and leave it.Never leave a fire unattended. Sparks or embers can blow into leaves or grass and ignite a fire that can quickly spread.The International Association of Fire Chiefs also advises you:Avoid using lawn mowers or other power equipment in dry vegetation.Don't drive through tall grass. Your vehicle's exhaust could spark a fire.Clear overgrown, dry vegetation from around your house.Wildfires burn in Texas, OklahomaPortions of Texas are under red flag warnings as wildfires burn through the Lone Star State, USA TODAY reports. In Oklahoma, wildfires have killed four people and injured at least 200, per USA TODAY.Low humidity is affecting South Central Texas as wildfires burn thousands of acres, according to the Texas A&M Forest Service Incident Viewer. The region faces \"critical fire weather conditions\" today as humidity levels are low, between 9% and 25%, and sustained winds of 15 to 25 mph, with some gusts reaching 35 mph sweep across the area, according to the National Weather Service.In Oklahoma, wildfires have destroyed almost 300 structures, including over 200 homes, and burned 179,000 acres, according to the Oklahoman, a part of the USA TODAY Network.",
